ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE FIRE-FIGHTING SYSTEM INSTALLATION BUSINESS ACT

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Fire-Fighting System Installation Business Act and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Registration Criteria and Scope of Business for Fire-Fighting System Businesses)
(1) The registration criteria and scope of business for each type of fire-fighting system business under Article 4 (1) and (2) of the Fire-Fighting System Installation Business Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") shall be as specified in attached Table 1.
(2) A person intending to register a fire-fighting system installation business shall meet the criteria in attached Table 1 and submit to the Special Metropolitan City Mayor, Integrated Special City Mayor, Metropolitan City Mayor, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Do Governor, or Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as "Mayor/Do Governor") a confirmation issued by a financial company designated by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency or the Fire Industry Mutual Aid Association under Article 23 of the Fire Industry Promotion Act, certifying that it has received collateral, a cash deposit, or a contribution in an amount equivalent to at least 20 percent of the required capital amount under attached Table 1. <Amended on November 19, 2014; June 22, 2015; July 26, 2017; June 23, 2026>
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor shall grant registration upon an application under Article 4 (1) of the Act, except where the application falls under any of the following: <Newly Inserted on December 13, 2011>
1. Where the registration criteria under paragraph (1) are not met;
2. Where the confirmation under paragraph (2) has not been submitted;
3. Where the applicant falls under any subparagraph of Article 5 of the Act;
4. Where the application otherwise violates a restriction under the Act, this Decree, or any other statute or regulation.

Article 2-2 (Exceptions for Temporary Failure to Meet Registration Criteria)
"Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as where a court decides to commence rehabilitation proceedings under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act and those proceedings are underway" in the proviso to Article 9 (1) 2 of the Act means any of the following cases: <Amended on April 29, 2016>
1. Where a listed company subject to the proviso to Article 542-8 (1) of the Commercial Act fails to meet the registration criteria for not more than 50 days due to a reduction in its total assets as of the end of the most recent business year;
2. Where the capital requirement among the registration criteria for each type of business under Article 2 (1) is not met and any of the following applies:
(a) Where a court has decided to commence rehabilitation proceedings under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act and those proceedings are underway;
(b) Where a court has determined that there is no impediment to implementing a rehabilitation plan and has decided to terminate rehabilitation proceedings for the relevant fire-fighting system business operator under the Debtor Rehabilitation and Bankruptcy Act, and the rehabilitation plan is being implemented;
(c) Where the council of financial creditors has resolved to commence joint management proceedings by the council of financial creditors under the Corporate Restructuring Promotion Act, and those proceedings are underway.

Article 2-3 (Qualifications of Persons Permitted to Conduct Performance-Based Design)
The qualifications and technical personnel required of persons permitted to conduct performance-based design under Article 11 (3) of the Act, and the scope of design permitted according to those qualifications, shall be as specified in attached Table 1-2.

Article 3 (Standards and Periods for Assignment of Fire-Fighting Engineers)
A person registered to engage in the fire-fighting system installation business pursuant to Article 4 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "installation contractor") shall assign its fire-fighting engineers to fire-fighting system installation sites in accordance with the assignment standards and periods in attached Table 2 pursuant to Article 12 (2) of the Act. <Amended on December 12, 2017>

Article 4 (Fire-Fighting System Installation Work Subject to Reporting of Commencement)
"Fire-fighting system installation work prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13 (1) of the Act means fire-fighting system installation work falling under any of the following: Provided, That fire-fighting system installation work at a manufacturing facility, etc. under Article 2 (1) 6 of the Safety Control of Hazardous Substances Act or at a publicly used establishment under Article 2 (1) 4 of the Special Act on the Safety Control of Publicly Used Establishments shall be excluded. <Amended on January 6, 2015; June 22, 2015; December 10, 2019; December 29, 2020; May 9, 2023; November 28, 2023; January 21, 2025>
1. Work to newly install any of the following systems in a specified fire-fighting object:
(a) Indoor fire hydrant systems (including hose-reel indoor fire hydrant systems; hereinafter the same shall apply), sprinkler systems, etc. (meaning sprinkler systems, etc. under subparagraph 1 (d) of attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Installation and Management of Fire-Fighting Systems; hereinafter the same shall apply), water spray and other fire-extinguishing systems (meaning water spray and other fire-extinguishing systems under subparagraph 1 (e) of attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Installation and Management of Fire-Fighting Systems; hereinafter the same shall apply), outdoor fire hydrant systems, fire-fighting water supply systems (excluding cases where these are installed by a mechanical equipment and gas installation contractor or waterworks and sewerage installation contractor under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry), smoke control systems (excluding cases where smoke control systems used concurrently for purposes other than fire safety are installed by a mechanical equipment and gas installation contractor under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry), standpipe systems, connected water-spray systems, or fire spread prevention systems;
(b) Emergency alarm systems, automatic fire detection systems, fire notification systems, emergency broadcasting systems (excluding cases where emergency broadcasting systems used concurrently for purposes other than fire safety are installed by an information and communications construction business operator under the Information and Communications Construction Business Act), emergency power outlet systems (excluding cases where these are installed by an electrical construction business operator under the Electrical Construction Business Act), or auxiliary radio communication systems (excluding cases where auxiliary radio communication systems used concurrently for purposes other than fire safety are installed by an information and communications construction business operator under the Information and Communications Construction Business Act);
2. Work to expand any of the following systems, zones, etc. in a specified fire-fighting object:
(a) Indoor or outdoor fire hydrant systems;
(b) Protection or water discharge zones of sprinkler systems, etc. or water spray and other fire-extinguishing systems; detection zones of automatic fire detection systems or fire notification systems; smoke control zones of smoke control systems (excluding cases where smoke control systems used concurrently for purposes other than fire safety are installed by a mechanical equipment and gas installation contractor under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry); water supply zones of standpipe systems; water-spray zones of connected water-spray systems; dedicated circuits of emergency power outlet systems; or water-spray zones of fire spread prevention systems;
3. Work to alter, relocate, or maintain all or part of any of the following components of fire-fighting systems, etc. installed in a specified fire-fighting object: Provided, That reporting may be omitted where urgent replacement or repair is required for a fire-fighting system that cannot be operated due to failure, damage, etc.:
(a) Fire alarm control panels;
(b) Fire pumps;
(c) Power control panels;
(d) Monitoring and control panels.

Article 5 (Scope of Specified Fire-Fighting Objects Subject to On-Site Confirmation for Completion Inspections)
"Specified fire-fighting objects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 14 (1) of the Act means the following among specified fire-fighting objects: <Amended on November 20, 2013; December 10, 2019>
1. Cultural and assembly facilities, religious facilities, sales facilities, facilities for older persons and children, training facilities, sports facilities, lodging facilities, warehouse facilities, underground shopping centers, and publicly used establishments under the Special Act on the Safety Control of Publicly Used Establishments;
2. Specified fire-fighting objects in which any of the following systems are installed:
(a) Sprinkler systems, etc.;
(b) Water spray and other fire-extinguishing systems (excluding hose-reel fire-extinguishing systems);
3. Specified fire-fighting objects with a total floor area of at least 10,000 square meters or at least 11 stories (excluding apartments);
4. Among facilities that manufacture, store, or handle combustible gas, facilities where the total storage capacity of combustible gas tanks exposed above ground is at least 1,000 tons.

Article 6 (Fire-Fighting Systems Subject to Defect Repairs and Defect Warranty Periods)
Fire-fighting systems for which defects must be repaired pursuant to Article 15 (1) of the Act, and the defect warranty period for each system, shall be according to the following classifications: <Amended on January 6, 2015; January 21, 2025>
1. Emergency alarm systems, emergency broadcasting systems, escape equipment, exit guidance lights, emergency lighting, and auxiliary radio communication systems: Two years;
2. Automatic fire-extinguishing devices, indoor fire hydrant systems, sprinkler systems, etc., water spray and other fire-extinguishing systems, outdoor fire hydrant systems, automatic fire detection systems, fire notification systems, fire-fighting water supply systems, and fire-fighting operations systems (excluding auxiliary radio communication systems): Three years.

Article 7 Deleted. <January 19, 2016>

Article 8 (Construction Sites for Fire-Fighting Objects Requiring Security, etc. That May Be Supervised by Persons Other Than Supervision Business Operators)
"Places prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 16 (2) of the Act means places where related facilities under Article 2, subparagraph 10 of the Nuclear Safety Act are installed. <Amended on October 25, 2011>

Article 9 (Types, Methods, and Scope of Fire-Fighting Installation Supervision)
The types, methods, and scope of fire-fighting installation supervision under Article 16 (3) of the Act shall be as specified in attached Table 3.

Article 10 (Scope of Specified Fire-Fighting Objects Requiring Designation of Construction Supervisors)
(1) "Specified fire-fighting objects prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 17 (1) of the Act means specified fire-fighting objects under Article 2 (1) 3 of the Act on Installation and Management of Fire-Fighting Systems. <Amended on June 1, 2021; November 29, 2022>
(2) "When installing fire-fighting systems prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as automatic fire detection systems or indoor fire hydrant systems" in Article 17 (1) of the Act means when installing fire-fighting systems falling under any of the following: <Amended on December 10, 2019; June 1, 2021; January 21, 2025>
1. When newly installing, altering, or expanding indoor fire hydrant systems;
2. When newly installing or altering sprinkler systems, etc. (excluding cabinet-type simplified sprinkler systems), or expanding their protection or water discharge zones;
3. When newly installing or altering water spray and other fire-extinguishing systems (excluding hose-reel fire-extinguishing systems), or expanding their protection or water discharge zones;
4. When newly installing, altering, or expanding outdoor fire hydrant systems;
5. When newly installing or altering automatic fire detection systems;
5-2. When newly installing or altering fire notification systems;
5-3. When newly installing or altering emergency broadcasting systems;
6. When newly installing or altering integrated monitoring facilities;
6-2. Deleted. <November 28, 2023>
7. When newly installing or altering fire-fighting water supply systems;
8. When carrying out the work specified in each of the following items for the corresponding fire-fighting operations systems:
(a) Newly installing or altering smoke control systems, or expanding smoke control zones;
(b) Newly installing or altering standpipe systems;
(c) Newly installing or altering connected water-spray systems, or expanding water supply zones;
(d) Newly installing or altering emergency power outlet systems, or expanding dedicated circuits;
(e) Newly installing or altering auxiliary radio communication systems;
(f) Newly installing or altering fire spread prevention systems, or expanding water-spray zones;
9. Deleted. <December 12, 2017>

Article 11 (Standards and Periods for Assignment of Fire-Fighting Installation Supervision Engineers)
A supervision business operator shall assign its supervision engineers to fire-fighting system installation sites in accordance with the assignment standards and periods in attached Table 4 pursuant to Article 18 (1) of the Act. <Amended on December 12, 2017>

Article 11-2 (Exceptions to Separate Contracting for Fire-Fighting System Installation Work)
"Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the proviso to Article 21 (2) of the Act means any of the following cases: <Amended on April 2, 2024; May 7, 2024>
1. Where work must urgently commence due to a disaster under Article 3, subparagraph 1 of the Framework Act on the Management of Disasters and Safety;
2. Where work must be kept confidential in connection with national defense, national security, etc.;
3. Where the work does not constitute fire-fighting system installation work under the subparagraphs of Article 4;
4. Where emergency alarm systems are installed in a specified fire-fighting object with a total floor area of not more than 1,000 square meters;
5. Where work is carried out through bidding falling under any of the following:
(a) Alternative bidding or turnkey bidding under Article 79 (1) 4 or 5 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party and Article 95 (1) 4 or 5 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which a Local Government Is a Party;
(b) Technical proposal bidding for detailed design or technical proposal bidding for basic design under Article 98, subparagraph 2 or 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which the State Is a Party and Article 127, subparagraph 2 or 3 of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Contracts to Which a Local Government Is a Party;
5-2. Where work concerns research or development facilities related to national high-tech strategic technologies under Article 2, subparagraph 1 of the Act on Special Measures for Strengthening the Competitiveness of and Protecting National High-Tech Strategic Industries, or facilities that manufacture products using such technologies;
6. Other work, such as national heritage repair, redevelopment, or reconstruction, which the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency recognizes as difficult to contract separately due to its nature.

Article 11-3 (Wages Excluded from Attachment)
The amount corresponding to wages that may not be attached pursuant to Article 21-2 of the Act shall be calculated by totaling the wages stated in the design documents out of the contract or subcontract amount for the relevant fire-fighting system installation work.

Article 11-4 (Contents of Contract Documents)
(1) "Other matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 21-3 (2) of the Act means the following matters: <Amended on December 24, 2019>
1. Details of the design, installation, and supervision of fire-fighting systems and flame-retardant treatment (hereinafter referred to as "fire-fighting system work, etc.");
2. The amount corresponding to wages out of the contract amount (including subcontract amounts; hereinafter the same shall apply in this paragraph);
3. The dates of commencement and completion of fire-fighting system work, etc.;
4. Where advance payments or progress payments of the contract amount are agreed upon, the time, method, and amount of each payment;
5. Matters concerning the allocation of losses where either party to the contract requests a design change, suspension of work, or cancellation of the contract;
6. Matters concerning the scope of exemption from liability due to natural disasters or other force majeure;
7. Matters concerning changes to the contract amount or details of fire-fighting system work, etc. due to design changes, price fluctuations, etc.;
8. Matters concerning issuance of a subcontract payment guarantee under Article 13-2 of the Fair Transactions in Subcontracting Act (applicable only to subcontracts);
9. Grounds and procedures for direct payment of subcontract consideration under Article 14 of the Fair Transactions in Subcontracting Act (applicable only to subcontracts);
10. Matters concerning payment of occupational safety and health management expenses under Article 72 of the Occupational Safety and Health Act (applicable only to the fire-fighting system installation business);
11. Matters concerning expenses borne pursuant to relevant statutes and regulations in connection with the work, such as insurance premiums under the Act on the Collection of Insurance Premiums, etc. for Employment Insurance and Industrial Accident Compensation Insurance, the National Pension Act, and the National Health Insurance Act (applicable only to the fire-fighting system installation business);
12. Inspections for delivery of the subject matter of the contract and the time of delivery;
13. The time for payment of the contract amount after completion of fire-fighting system work, etc.;
14. Matters concerning compensation for damage where performance of the contract is delayed, such as payment of contractual penalties and interest for delay;
15. Fire-fighting systems subject to defect repairs, defect warranty periods, and methods of providing a warranty against defects (applicable only to the fire-fighting system installation business);
16. Matters concerning methods of disposal and recycling of waste generated by the work (applicable only to the fire-fighting system installation business);
17. Other matters specified pursuant to other statutes and regulations or by agreement between both parties to the contract.
(2) To enable contracting parties to conclude contracts fairly on an equal footing, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency may establish and distribute standard contracts for contracts or subcontracts concerning fire-fighting system work, etc. (in the case of subcontracts, meaning the standard subcontract for the fire-fighting system installation business recommended by the Fair Trade Commission under the Fair Transactions in Subcontracting Act). <Amended on July 26, 2017>

Article 11-5 (Scope of Public Institutions Exempt from Guarantees of Payment of Construction Consideration)
"Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the main clause of Article 21-4 (1) of the Act means the following public institutions:
1. Public enterprises and quasi-governmental institutions under Article 5 of the Act on the Management of Public Institutions;
2. Local public corporations under Article 49 of the Local Public Enterprises Act and local public agencies under Article 76 of that Act.

Article 11-6 (Scope of Fire-Fighting System Installation Work Exempt from Guarantees of Payment of Construction Consideration)
"Fire-fighting system installation work prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as small-scale work" in Article 21-4 (2) of the Act means the following fire-fighting system installation work:
1. Small-scale fire-fighting system installation work with a contract amount of less than 10 million won per project;
2. Short-term fire-fighting system installation work with a construction period of not more than three months.

Article 11-7 (Scope of Public Institutions Subject to the Prohibition on Acquiring or Providing Property, etc. through Improper Solicitation)
"Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 21-5 (2) and (3) of the Act means, respectively, the public institutions under the subparagraphs of Article 11-5.

Article 11-8 (Scope of Public Institutions to Be Notified of Violations)
"Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 21-6 of the Act means the public institutions under the subparagraphs of Article 11-5.

Article 12 (Cases Where Fire-Fighting System Installation Work May Be Subcontracted)
(1) Where an installation contractor engaged in both the fire-fighting system installation business and any of the following businesses is awarded a contract for both fire-fighting system installation work and work for the relevant business, the installation contractor may subcontract part of the contracted fire-fighting system installation work to another installation contractor pursuant to the proviso of Article 22 (1) of the Act. <Amended 2016.8.11, 2020.9.8, 2021.6.1>
1. A housing construction business under Article 4 of the Housing Act;
2. A construction business under Article 9 of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry;
3. An electrical construction business under Article 4 of the Electrical Construction Business Act;
4. An information and communications construction business under Article 14 of the Information and Communications Construction Business Act.
(2) Fire-fighting system installation work, part of which an installation contractor may subcontract to another installation contractor pursuant to paragraph (1), shall be work to install 1 or more of the fire-fighting systems specified in the items of subparagraph 1 of Article 4. <Amended 2021.6.1>

Article 12-2 (Review of Appropriateness of Subcontracts, etc.)
(1) "Where the subcontract amount is less than an amount calculated at the ratio prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 22-2 (1) of the Act means any of the following cases:
1. Where the subcontract amount is less than 82/100 of the amount corresponding to the subcontracted portion of the contract amount [meaning the amount calculated on the basis of the contract unit prices (including direct and indirect labor costs, material costs, and expenses) in the contractor's itemized breakdown of the contract amount for the fire-fighting system work, etc. to be subcontracted, plus general administrative expenses, profit, and value-added tax, but excluding amounts borne by the contractor pursuant to relevant statutes and regulations, such as the costs of materials supplied directly by the contractor to the subcontractor];
2. Where the subcontract amount is less than 60/100 of the project owner's estimated price for the fire-fighting system work, etc.
(2) "Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter part of Article 22-2 (1) of the Act means the public institutions specified in the subparagraphs of Article 11-5. <Amended 2022.1.4>
1. Deleted <2022.1.4>
2. Deleted <2022.1.4>
(3) The Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall determine and publicly notify criteria that may be used when reviewing the subcontractor's construction and performance capabilities, the appropriateness of the contents of the subcontract, etc. pursuant to Article 22-2 (1) of the Act. <Amended 2017.7.26>
(4) Where a project owner intends to request a change of subcontractor or a change to the contents of a subcontract pursuant to Article 22-2 (2) of the Act, the project owner shall do so in writing within 30 days from the date on which the project owner was notified of matters concerning the subcontract pursuant to Article 21-3 (4) of the Act or became aware that grounds for such a request existed.

Article 12-3 (Composition and Operation of Subcontract Review Committees)
(1) A Subcontract Review Committee under Article 22-2 (4) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall consist of not more than 10 members, including 1 chairperson and 1 vice-chairperson.
(2) The chairperson of the Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "chairperson") shall be the head of the contracting agency (meaning, where the contracting agency is a Special Metropolitan City, Integrated Special City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, or Special Self-Governing Province, a person designated by the head of the contracting agency from among its public officials of Grade II or III, and where the contracting agency is a public institution specified in the subparagraphs of Article 11-5, a person designated by the head of the contracting agency from among its executive officers and employees of Grade I or higher), and the vice-chairperson and members shall be appointed, or commissioned in consideration of gender, by the chairperson from among persons falling under any of the following subparagraphs: <Amended 2022.1.4, 2026.6.23>
1. Public officials of director level or higher of the relevant contracting agency (meaning executive officers and employees of Grade II or higher in the case of public institutions specified in the subparagraphs of Article 11-5);
2. Persons of research fellow level or higher at research institutions in the field of fire protection;
3. Persons who have obtained a doctoral degree in the field of fire protection and have at least 3 years of research or practical experience in that field;
4. Persons who are assistant professors or higher at universities (limited to the field of fire protection);
5. Persons who have obtained qualifications as a professional engineer in fire protection under the National Technical Qualifications Act.
(3) The term of office of members falling under paragraph (2) 2 through 5 shall be 3 years, and they may be reappointed for 1 consecutive term only.
(4) A meeting of the Committee shall be convened with the attendance of a majority of all incumbent members, and resolutions shall be passed with the affirmative vote of a majority of the members present.
(5) Matters necessary for the operation of the Committee, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (4), shall be determined by the chairperson following a resolution of the Committee.

Article 12-4 (Exclusion, Challenge, and Recusal of Committee Members)
(1) A member of the Committee shall be excluded from the review of the relevant subcontract if the member falls under any of the following subparagraphs:
1. Where the member or the member's spouse or former spouse is a party to the agenda item concerned (including an executive officer of a party where the party is a corporation, organization, etc.; hereinafter the same shall apply in this subparagraph and subparagraph 2) or is a joint holder of rights or a joint obligor with a party to that agenda item;
2. Where the member is or was a relative of a party to the agenda item concerned;
3. Where the member has made a statement or conducted an appraisal in relation to the agenda item concerned;
4. Where the member or a corporation, organization, etc. to which the member belongs is or was an agent of a party to the agenda item concerned;
5. Where the member was involved in the disposition or omission that gave rise to the agenda item concerned.
(2) Where circumstances exist that make it difficult to expect an impartial review from a member, a party to the relevant agenda item may file a challenge with the Committee, and the Committee shall decide on it by resolution. In such cases, the member against whom the challenge is filed shall not participate in that resolution.
(3) Where a member falls under any of the grounds for exclusion specified in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1), the member shall recuse himself or herself from the review of the relevant agenda item.

Article 12-5 (Disclosure of Subcontract Data)
(1) "Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the portion of Article 22-4 (1) of the Act other than its subparagraphs means the public institutions specified in the subparagraphs of Article 11-5. <Amended 2022.1.4>
(2) Disclosure of subcontract data for fire-fighting system work, etc. under Article 22-4 (1) of the Act shall be made by posting the data on the website of the agency that ordered the relevant fire-fighting system work, etc. within 30 days from the date on which it was notified of matters concerning the subcontract pursuant to Article 21-3 (4) of the Act.
(3) The scale of contracts subject to disclosure of subcontract data for fire-fighting system work, etc. under Article 22-4 (1) of the Act shall be those where the subcontract amount [meaning the amount calculated on the basis of the contract unit prices (including direct and indirect labor costs, material costs, and expenses) in the subcontractor's itemized breakdown of the subcontract amount, plus general administrative expenses, profit, and value-added tax, but excluding amounts borne by the contractor pursuant to relevant statutes and regulations, such as the costs of materials supplied directly by the contractor to the subcontractor] is at least 10 million won.

Article 12-6 (Entities Required to Prepare and Publicly Announce Execution Plans for Design and Construction Supervision Service Projects)
"Public institutions prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the former part of Article 26-2 (1) of the Act means the public institutions specified in the subparagraphs of Article 11-5. <Amended 2022.1.4>

Article 12-7 (Contents of Execution Plans for Design and Construction Supervision Service Projects, etc.)
(1) An execution plan under the former part of Article 26-2 (1) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended 2022.1.4>
1. The name of the design and construction supervision services;
2. The name of the agency implementing the design and construction supervision service project;
3. The principal details of the design and construction supervision service project;
4. Total project costs and the budget amount for the relevant year;
5. The expected timing of bidding;
6. Other matters necessary for participation in bidding.
(2) An execution plan under the former part of Article 26-2 (1) of the Act may be publicly announced together with the invitation to bid. <Amended 2022.1.4>

Article 12-8 (Procedures for Selection of Design and Supervision Business Operators, etc.)
(1) "Criteria for evaluating project performance capability prescribed by Presidential Decree" in the latter part of Article 26-2 (1) of the Act means criteria for evaluating the following matters: <Amended 2017.7.26, 2022.1.4>
1. The performance records and experience of participating fire-fighting engineers;
2. Creditworthiness evaluated on the basis of whether dispositions such as restrictions on participation in bidding or suspension of business have been imposed, the soundness of financial condition, etc.;
3. Records of technology development and investment;
4. The degree of overlapping assignments of participating fire-fighting engineers;
5. Other matters prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety.
(2) Where the State, a local government, or a public institution under Article 12-6 (hereinafter referred to as the "State, etc."; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) orders design or construction supervision services for fire-fighting systems publicly announced pursuant to the former part of Article 26-2 (1) of the Act (including cases where a designating authority for supervision business operators under Article 12-9 (2) publicly announces an invitation to apply in order to select a supervision business operator), it shall evaluate persons who intend to participate in bidding in accordance with the criteria for evaluating project performance capability under paragraph (1) and select persons to participate in bidding. <Amended 2022.1.4, 2025.1.21>
(3) Where the State, etc. intends to select a person with particularly outstanding technical capability as the successful bidder when ordering design or construction supervision services for fire-fighting systems, it may require the persons selected to participate in bidding pursuant to paragraph (2) to submit separate technical and price bids, evaluate technical capability first, and then select the successful bidder by negotiating with firms in descending order of their technical capability evaluation scores.
(4) Details necessary for the selection of design and supervision business operators, such as detailed criteria and methods for evaluating project performance capability, criteria and methods for evaluating technical capability, and negotiation methods under paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended 2017.7.26>

Article 12-9 (Scale and Types of Housing Construction Work for Which Supervision Business Operators Are to Be Selected, etc.)
(1) The scale and types of housing construction work for which a supervision business operator must be selected pursuant to the former part of Article 26-2 (2) of the Act shall be multi-family housing under the Housing Act (excluding dormitories) comprising at least 300 households. <Amended 2025.1.21>
(2) Where a person falling under any of the following subparagraphs (hereinafter referred to as a "designating authority for supervision business operators") intends to select a supervision business operator pursuant to the former part of Article 26-2 (2) of the Act, the person shall, within 7 days from the date on which a housing construction project plan was approved pursuant to Article 15 (1) of the Housing Act (hereinafter referred to as a "housing construction project plan" in this Article) or a project implementation plan was authorized pursuant to Article 50 (1) of the Act on the Improvement of Urban Areas and Residential Environments (hereinafter referred to as a "project implementation plan" in this Article), publicly announce an invitation for supervision business operators to supervise fire-fighting system installation work, separately from other construction work. <Amended 2025.1.21>
1. Where a housing construction project plan is approved: The Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun;
2. Where a project implementation plan is authorized: The Special Self-Governing City Mayor, the Special Self-Governing Province Governor, or the head of a Si, Gun, or autonomous Gu.
(3) Notwithstanding paragraph (2), where a project entity makes a request due to unavoidable grounds, such as an extension of the period for commencing construction under Article 31 of the Enforcement Decree of the Housing Act, the designating authority for supervision business operators may publicly announce the invitation under paragraph (2) within 7 days from the date on which those grounds cease to exist. <Amended 2025.1.21>
(4) A public invitation under paragraph (2) shall include the following matters:
1. The application period;
2. The method of determining the successful bidder;
3. The details of the project and documents to be submitted;
4. The reference date for determining the eligibility of supervision engineer applicants (which shall, in principle, be the application deadline);
5. The reference date for determining the performance records of supervision business operators and the experience of supervision engineers (which shall, in principle, be the date of the public invitation);
6. Matters concerning electronic processing of bidding;
7. Other matters necessary for inviting applications from supervision business operators.
(5) A public invitation under paragraph (2) shall be made by such methods as publication in a daily newspaper or posting for at least 7 days on the bulletin board and website of the relevant Special Metropolitan City, Integrated Special City, Metropolitan City, Special Self-Governing City, Do, Special Self-Governing Province, Si/Gun, or autonomous Gu. <Amended 2025.1.21, 2026.6.23>

Article 13 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 14 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 15 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 16 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 17 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 18 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 19 Deleted <2015.6.22>

Article 19-2 (Procedures for Authorization of Establishment of the Fire-Fighting System Business Operators Association, etc.)
(1) To establish the Fire-Fighting System Business Operators Association pursuant to Article 30-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Association"), at least 10 fire-fighting system business operators under Article 2 (1) 2 of the Act shall act as promoters, adopt the articles of incorporation at an inaugural general meeting, and then apply for authorization to the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency. <Amended 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26>
(2) Upon granting authorization under paragraph (1), the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall publicly announce that fact. <Amended 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26>

Article 19-3 (Matters to Be Stated in Articles of Incorporation)
The articles of incorporation of the Association shall include the following matters:
1. Purpose;
2. Name;
3. Location of the principal office;
4. Matters concerning its business;
5. Matters concerning the admission and withdrawal of members;
6. Matters concerning membership dues;
7. Matters concerning assets and accounting;
8. The number, terms of office, and methods of election of executive officers;
9. Matters concerning its organs and organization;
10. Matters concerning the general meeting and board of directors;
11. Matters concerning amendments to the articles of incorporation.

Article 19-4 (Supervision)
Pursuant to Article 30-2 (4) of the Act, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency may require the Association to report on the following matters:
1. Important matters resolved by the general meeting or board of directors;
2. Matters concerning the admission and withdrawal of members and membership dues;
3. Other important matters concerning the Association and its members.

Article 20 (Entrustment of Duties)
(1) Pursuant to Article 33 (2) of the Act, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall entrust duties concerning practical training of fire-fighting engineers under Article 29 of the Act to a practical training institution designated by the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency pursuant to Article 29 (3) of the Act or to the Korea Fire Safety Institute under Article 40 of the Framework Act on Firefighting Services. <Amended 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26, 2018.6.26>
(2) Pursuant to Article 33 (3) of the Act, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall entrust the following duties to the Association: <Amended 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26, 2019.2.8, 2020.9.8>
1. Duties concerning the evaluation and public disclosure of flame-retardant treatment capability under Article 20-3 of the Act;
2. Duties concerning the evaluation and public disclosure of construction capability under Article 26 of the Act;
3. Establishment and operation of the integrated information system for fire-fighting system businesses under Article 26-3 (1) of the Act.
(3) Pursuant to Article 33 (3) of the Act, Mayors/Do Governors shall entrust the following duties to the Association: <Added 2015.6.22, 2016.7.28>
1. Receipt of applications for registration of fire-fighting system businesses under Article 4 (1) of the Act and verification of the contents of applications;
2. Receipt of reports of changes to registered matters of fire-fighting system businesses under Article 6 of the Act and verification of the contents of reports;
2-2. Receipt of reports of temporary closure, permanent closure, or reopening of fire-fighting system businesses under Article 6-2 of the Act and verification of the contents of reports;
3. Receipt of reports of succession to the status of fire-fighting system business operators under Article 7 (3) of the Act and verification of the contents of reports.
(4) Pursuant to Article 33 (4) of the Act, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall entrust the following duties to the Association or a corporation or organization related to fire-fighting technology. In such cases, the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency shall designate and publicly notify the entrusted institution. <Amended 2014.11.19, 2015.6.22, 2017.7.26, 2022.1.4>
1. Duties concerning the recognition of qualifications, academic backgrounds, and experience related to fire-fighting technology under Article 28 of the Act;
2. Duties concerning education and training for the development and recognition of fire-fighting engineers under Article 28-2 of the Act.

Article 20-2 (Processing of Unique Identifying Information)
The Commissioner of the National Fire Agency (including persons entrusted with the duties of the Commissioner of the National Fire Agency pursuant to Article 20), a Mayor/Do Governor (including persons to whom the relevant authority has been delegated or entrusted, where such authority has been delegated or entrusted), the head of a fire headquarters, or the chief of a fire station may process materials containing resident registration numbers or alien registration numbers under subparagraph 1 or 4 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act where it is unavoidable for the performance of the following affairs: <Amended 2014.11.19, 2016.7.28, 2017.7.26, 2021.6.1, 2022.1.4>
1. Affairs concerning registration of fire-fighting system businesses under Article 4 (1) of the Act;
1-2. Affairs concerning verification of grounds for disqualification from registration under Article 5 of the Act;
1-3. Affairs concerning reports of changes to registered matters of fire-fighting system businesses under Article 6 of the Act;
1-4. Affairs concerning reports of succession to the status of fire-fighting system business operators under Article 7 of the Act;
2. Affairs concerning revocation of registration, suspension of business, etc. under Article 9 (1) of the Act;
3. Affairs concerning dispositions imposing penalty surcharges under Article 10 of the Act;
3-2. Affairs concerning the evaluation and public disclosure of construction capability under Article 26 of the Act;
4. Affairs concerning the recognition, etc. of qualifications, academic backgrounds, and experience related to fire-fighting technology under Article 28 (1) of the Act;
4-2. Affairs concerning education and training for the development and recognition of fire-fighting engineers under Article 28-2 (1) of the Act;
5. Affairs concerning practical training of fire-fighting engineers under Article 29 (1) of the Act;
6. Affairs concerning supervision under Article 31 of the Act.

